Subject: Never compose a YUV ColorBuffer that holds no finished upload

A YUV ColorBuffer holds what a guest CPU wrote into a gralloc buffer (a video decoder's
pictures, a camera's frames), and only an upload writes it: the guest packs the planes, and
updateColorBufferFromBytesLocked copies them in, one GPU job per plane. The compositor (patch
0040) sampled such a ColorBuffer whatever its uploads had done. Read as YUV, a luma and chroma
of 0 is solid green, and on the owner's PC (Intel Arc, 1.0.1-dev.25) YouTube Shorts still
flickered green while the host decoder (`0 not written by the decoder`) and the hwcomposer's
upload (`kept from the host` 0) both reported every picture whole.

Three ways the composition could read zeros, none of which the guest's checks see:

- A ColorBuffer no upload has finished in holds its allocation's zeros: a new one, or one made
  again on a new device after a GPU reset, which the guest still believes holds its last
  picture.
- An upload whose later job failed (a submit refused, a fence not signaled in time) left one
  plane new and the other old or zero. It also left the bookkeeping wrong: the first job takes
  the image to the transfer layout on the renderer's queue, and only the last gives it back,
  but currentLayout still said where the image had been (or, when the last job was recorded and
  not submitted, where it would have gone), so the next composition's barrier started from a
  layout the image was not in.
- A guest Vulkan driver in any process but SurfaceFlinger copies up, before each of its
  submissions, every buffer a CPU writes that it has imported (guest/mesa 0008), whoever owns
  the buffer then. It can copy a video buffer its decoder has not written yet, and overwrite the
  picture the hwcomposer uploaded just before the composition with zeros. Only the hwcomposer
  checks its planes for a picture.

Now each ColorBuffer records what its uploads left: none, part of one, or a whole picture
(dsa_yuv_pictures.h). An upload counts its submitted and finished jobs; a failed one leaves the
picture part-written and the layout and queue family where the GPU left the image. A
composition with a layer of a YUV ColorBuffer that holds no whole picture is refused before it is
scheduled, so rcComposeWithoutPost answers 0, the hwcomposer posts nothing for that frame, and
the task's window keeps its last frame with the last picture that did land. An upload of YUV
planes that hold no picture (a sampled luma row of zeros over chroma zeros, which no encoder
makes) is refused, and the ColorBuffer keeps the picture it had. Both are counted in a renderer
warning at most every 5 s:

  video pictures: the task window kept its last frame instead of N composition(s) whose video
  ColorBuffer held no finished upload (A never written, B left part-written), and M upload(s)
  whose planes held no picture (zeros) were refused, ...

